---
// Privacy Policy for the Paths app. Written from what the app and its backend
// do (Paths repo: privacy manifest, Info.plist, Supabase migrations and edge
// functions, the Arete client, onboarding contacts, account deletion), not
// from intentions. When the app's data handling changes, change this page.
//
// The facts it states are checked against the app and the backend: the database
// and files are in Supabase's eu-west-1 (Ireland), Arete's requests go to
// Anthropic in the United States, account deletion removes uploaded files, and
// proof files are private and served through links that expire.
//
// Kept out of search results (noindex) until the author approves the text.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import LegalPage from "../components/LegalPage.astro";
---

<Layout title="Privacy Policy" description="What Paths collects, why, and what you can do about it.">
  <LegalPage title="Privacy Policy">
    <span slot="updated">8 November 2026</span>

    <p>
      This policy explains what the Paths app collects, why, who else handles it, and what you can do about it. Paths
      runs on iPhone, iPad and Apple Watch. It is made by Benjamin Jack Laubacher ("we", "us"), who can be
      reached at <a href="mailto:support@particlesofmind.com">support@particlesofmind.com</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="summary">The short version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>We collect what Paths needs to run your account, your patterns and the proof you submit.</li>
      <li>We do not sell your data, show ads, or track you across other apps and websites.</li>
      <li>What you publish in Paths is seen by your friends in Paths.</li>
      <li>Messages to Arete go to Anthropic to write a reply. Our server does not keep them.</li>
      <li>You can delete your account in the app.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="collect">What we collect, and why</h2>

    <h3>Your account</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign-in.</strong> If you sign up with email, your email address and password. The password is stored
        only in hashed form. If you use Sign in with Apple, Apple gives us an identifier and the email address you
        choose to share, which can be a private relay address.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Profile.</strong> Your name, date of birth, gender, the path (archetype) you choose, a username the app
        creates for you, the date you joined, and when you agreed to the Terms.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We use these to run your account, to check that you are at least 16, to show your name and username to your
      friends, and to give Arete context.
    </p>

    <h3>Your patterns and proof</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Patterns.</strong> What you schedule: each pattern's name, method, days and times, time zone and
        reminder settings, and every completion, skip and extra time you use.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Proof.</strong> What a method asks for when you complete it. Depending on the method, that is a photo you
        take with the camera, a drawing you make in the app, an audio recording, the text or result the method produces,
        or a tracked walk or run.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Walks and runs.</strong> While you track one, Paths records your route with GPS, and your distance,
        speed, duration, steps and floors climbed. An estimated calorie figure is saved with it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Exercise counts.</strong> For push-ups, squats and planks, the camera watches your body position and the
        app counts on your device. Only the count or the time is saved. The camera images are not uploaded.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reflections and progress.</strong> Your answers to Brief and Debrief questions, reports Arete writes
        about your badges, and your points, levels, badges, streaks and PATH scores.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>We use these to show your schedule, record and check completions, work out your progress, and show your friends what you publish.</p>

    <h3>Friends and invitations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Friends.</strong> Who you have added, who you have asked, who has asked you, and who you have blocked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Feedback.</strong> Reactions, reviews, awards and challenges you give or receive, and the notices about
        them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Contacts.</strong> If you allow it, Paths reads the names and phone numbers in your contacts on your
        device so you can pick people. For each person you pick, we store only a fingerprint (a one-way hash) of the
        last eight digits of their number, never their name or number. We use it only to recognise them if they join
        Paths. Arete is told only how many people you invited, never who.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Invitations.</strong> An invitation is sent from your own Messages app or share sheet, with your name and
        your invite link. We do not send it. If someone joins through your link, we record that you invited them and
        when they complete their first pattern.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reports and suggestions.</strong> The Report button on a post opens an email to us with that post's
        username, pattern and date. If you send a report or a feature suggestion, we receive what you write, and for
        a report your email address.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Subscriptions</h3>
    <p>
      You buy Plus or Max through Apple's App Store. We never see your payment details. We store which plan you have,
      whether it is active, the App Store product, and when it last changed.
    </p>

    <h3>Arete</h3>
    <p>When you write to Arete, the app sends your message and recent messages from the chat. Unless you turn off
      <strong>Use my Paths activity</strong> in Configure Arete, it also sends your name, username, path, gender, age,
      join date, schedule, progress, PATH scores, recent completions and latest Brief and Debrief answers. It always
      sends your plan, your device type and your language.</p>
    <p>
      If you turn on <strong>Remember things I share</strong>, Arete saves lasting facts you tell it and sends them with
      each message, along with up to 14 days of earlier chats. You can review and delete these facts in Configure Arete
      &rarr; Memory.
    </p>
    <p>
      The request goes to our server, which checks that you are signed in and within your daily limit, then passes it
      to Anthropic, which writes the reply. Our server does not keep your messages or Arete's replies. It logs technical
      details only: which model answered, how many tokens were used, how long it took, and whether it worked. It also
      counts your requests each day.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your chat history and Arete's saved facts are stored on your device, under your account, not on our servers.
      Anthropic processes the requests under its own terms, does not use them to train its models, and keeps them
      only for the short period those terms allow.
    </p>
    <p>Do not share sensitive information with Arete, such as financial details, ID numbers or medical records.</p>

    <h3>Health and motion</h3>
    <p>
      During a tracked walk or run, Paths uses your device's motion sensors for steps and floors, and may read steps
      and walking and running distance from Apple Health. With your permission it reads your latest body weight from
      Apple Health to estimate calories. Your weight stays on your device; only the estimate is saved. On Apple Watch, a
      tracked run is saved as a workout in Apple Health. We never use Health data for advertising.
    </p>

    <h3>Usage and diagnostics</h3>
    <p>
      The app records events about how it is used and how it is running: for example opening an offer, publishing a
      completion, an error, or the app closing unexpectedly. Each event carries your user ID, a session ID, the app
      version and the platform. These go to our own database. We use no third-party analytics, advertising or crash
      reporting services. We use the events to fix problems and improve Paths.
    </p>

    <h2 id="permissions">What the app asks to use on your device</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Camera:</strong> to take proof photos and count exercises.</li>
      <li><strong>Microphone:</strong> to record audio proof.</li>
      <li><strong>Photos:</strong> to choose a reference image for drawing.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Location:</strong> only during a walk or run you start, including with the screen off if you allow it.
        Paths does not collect your location at other times.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Motion and Apple Health:</strong> as described above.</li>
      <li><strong>Contacts:</strong> to pick friends to invite, as described above.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notifications and alarms:</strong> reminders and wake-up alarms are scheduled on your device. Paths does
        not use push notifications.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each is optional. You can turn it off at any time in your device's Settings; the methods that need it will stop working.</p>
    <p>
      Your Apple Watch and the Paths widgets exchange your schedule and proof with your iPhone directly, on your own
      devices.
    </p>

    <h2 id="who">Who can see your information</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Your friends in Paths</strong> see your username and profile, your progress and badges, and the
        completions you publish with their proof: photos, drawings, recordings and route maps.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Other people with a Paths account</strong> can reach the profiles and pattern records the app
        shows around the product &mdash; crews, rankings and the feed are built out of them. Proof files are not
        public: each one is served through a link that is made for the reader and expires within the hour.
      </li>
      <li><strong>We</strong> can access your data to run, secure and support Paths.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Our service providers</strong> handle data for us and for no other purpose:
        <ul>
          <li>
            Supabase stores the database and files, runs sign-in and our server functions, and sends account emails
            such as password resets.
          </li>
          <li>Anthropic writes Arete's replies.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple</strong> handles Sign in with Apple, App Store purchases, Apple Health and the maps in the app,
        under Apple's own privacy policy.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Project Gutenberg</strong>, through Gutendex, supplies the public-domain books you read in Paths. Those
        requests carry your book search, not your account.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>We do not sell or rent your data, and we share it with no one else unless the law requires us to.</p>

    <h2 id="where">Where your data is stored</h2>
    <p>
      Our database and files are hosted by Supabase in Ireland, in the European Union. Arete's requests are processed by
      Anthropic in the United States. That transfer is covered by the standard contractual clauses in our agreement
      with Anthropic, which is the safeguard European law asks for.
    </p>
    <p>Data travels encrypted between the app and our servers. Only you can change your own records.</p>

    <h2 id="keep">How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>We keep your account data for as long as you have an account. Separately:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Usage and diagnostic events: kept while they are useful for finding problems. Deleting your account unlinks
        them from you, and what is left names no one.
      </li>
      <li>Server logs, including Arete's technical logs: kept by Supabase for days, not months, and never exported.</li>
      <li>Backups: Supabase takes them daily and rolls them over, so deleted data leaves them as they turn over.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="delete">Deleting your account</h2>
    <p>
      You can delete your account in the app: in Profile, open Settings (the gear) and choose Delete account. This deletes your
      account, profile, patterns and completions, progress, reflections, friends list, notices sent to you, invite
      records, challenges and feature suggestions.
    </p>
    <p>Some things remain:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Usage and diagnostic events are kept, but no longer linked to your account.</li>
      <li>Feedback and reactions you left on friends' completions stay in their records.</li>
      <li>Notices your actions sent to friends stay with them, no longer linked to your account.</li>
      <li>
        The files you uploaded &mdash; proof photos, drawings and recordings &mdash; are deleted with the
        account.
      </li>
      <li>
        Arete's chat history and saved facts are on your device. Deleting the app removes them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Deleting your account does not cancel a subscription bought through Apple. To cancel, open the Settings app on
      your iPhone, tap your name, then Subscriptions, and choose Paths. Before you delete your account you can also use
      Manage or cancel subscription in the app's subscription screen.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rights">Your choices and rights</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        You can see your profile details in the app's Settings, and download them there with Download data. That file
        holds your basic profile, not your patterns or proof.
      </li>
      <li>You can turn device permissions and Arete's settings on and off at any time.</li>
      <li>
        You can ask us for a full copy of your data, to correct or delete it, or to stop or limit how we use it. Write
        to support@particlesofmind.com.
      </li>
      <li>You can complain to your data protection authority.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Where the law asks for a legal basis: we use your data to provide Paths under our agreement with you; we use your
      contacts, location, camera, microphone, Health data and Arete's memory only with your permission, which you can
      withdraw; and we use diagnostics in our legitimate interest in keeping Paths working and secure.
    </p>

    <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
    <p>
      Paths is for people aged 16 and over, and the app checks age at sign-up. If you believe someone younger has an
      account, write to us and we will delete it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="website">This website</h2>
    <p>
      particlesofmind.com has no accounts and runs no analytics or advertising scripts. Its host,
      Squarespace, may keep standard server logs such as IP addresses.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      When we change this policy we update the date above. If a change is material, we will tell you in the app or by
      email before it takes effect.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Benjamin Jack Laubacher<br />
      <a href="mailto:support@particlesofmind.com">support@particlesofmind.com</a>
    </p>
    <p>Our Terms of Use are at <a href="/terms">particlesofmind.com/terms</a>.</p>
  </LegalPage>
</Layout>
